00:00:20.583 --> 00:00:22.208
This is

00:00:22.208 --> 00:00:25.458
The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:29.792 --> 00:00:31.959
Hey everybody,
this is Gary Vay-ner-chuk

00:00:31.959 --> 00:00:33.875
and this is episode 255,

00:00:33.875 --> 00:00:37.875
we think, of
The #AskGaryVee Show.

00:00:37.875 --> 00:00:40.625
We are in a beautiful
setting here in Cannes

00:00:40.625 --> 00:00:42.792
at the Advertising Festival

00:00:42.792 --> 00:00:44.250
but way more important than that

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.125
we're hanging with
some of our favorite friends

00:00:46.125 --> 00:00:48.250
but way more
important than that,

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:52.333
we have music superstar
Black Coffee in the house

00:00:52.333 --> 00:00:55.500
with his epic light blue pants

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.875
and his massive skills.

00:00:57.875 --> 00:00:58.583
Black Coffee, my man.

00:00:58.583 --> 00:01:01.667
First of all, great to see you.

00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:02.750
Why don't you
tell the Vayner Nation

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:03.834
that's watching right now,

00:01:03.834 --> 00:01:05.750
for the people that
have been living under a rock

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:07.333
before we get into the questions

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:08.333
that are coming
through Facebook.

00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:10.583
Great to see you start
firing away your questions.

00:01:10.583 --> 00:01:12.708
Why don't you tell
for a minute or two,

00:01:12.708 --> 00:01:15.000
not only what you do but a
little bit of the backstory

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.458
which I'm very fond of,

00:01:16.458 --> 00:01:18.458
of how you've
gotten to this point

00:01:18.458 --> 00:01:19.458
and what you do for a living.

00:01:19.458 --> 00:01:20.583
So fire away.

00:01:20.583 --> 00:01:23.542
- I'm basically, I'm a music guy

00:01:23.542 --> 00:01:26.291
born in South Africa.

00:01:26.291 --> 00:01:29.500
Studied music, dropped out,

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.542
started a band, dropped out,

00:01:33.208 --> 00:01:35.208
started my solo career

00:01:35.208 --> 00:01:38.458
as a music producer and a DJ.

00:01:38.458 --> 00:01:40.875
Been doing this
for over 10 years now.

00:01:40.875 --> 00:01:42.291
Yeah.

00:01:42.291 --> 00:01:44.041
- That's how, that's it?
- That's it.

00:01:44.041 --> 00:01:45.875
- So on a very serious note,

00:01:45.875 --> 00:01:48.208
what's really interesting is

00:01:48.208 --> 00:01:50.458
I'm fortunate enough to hang
out with a lot of fun people

00:01:50.458 --> 00:01:52.834
but the selfie that you
and I took 18 months ago,

00:01:52.834 --> 00:01:55.792
the passion of your
audience is so incredible.

00:01:55.792 --> 00:01:57.333
When people saw that we
were hanging out together,

00:01:57.333 --> 00:01:58.166
they were like,

00:01:58.166 --> 00:01:59.875
it blew their mind

00:01:59.875 --> 00:02:01.667
and I have been following along
since the day I've met you

00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:03.208
with your career and
things of that nature.

00:02:03.208 --> 00:02:06.041
It's incredible
to watch the passion

00:02:06.041 --> 00:02:07.542
that your fan base has.

00:02:07.542 --> 00:02:09.917
Why do you think
you've broken out

00:02:09.917 --> 00:02:13.166
and actually because again
I wanna make sure people know.

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:15.542
How do you define your genre?

00:02:15.542 --> 00:02:17.375
'Cause I think you do
it a little bit different

00:02:17.375 --> 00:02:19.875
and I wanna just hear
it from the horse's mouth.

00:02:19.875 --> 00:02:23.041
How do you define your
style of DJing and music?

00:02:23.041 --> 00:02:25.458
- I just call it house music.

00:02:26.625 --> 00:02:30.333
One guy literally
yesterday was asking me,

00:02:30.333 --> 00:02:32.291
"I know it's house
but what kind of house?"

00:02:32.291 --> 00:02:34.875
And I try not to go there.

00:02:34.875 --> 00:02:36.125
- On purpose?
- Yeah.

00:02:36.125 --> 00:02:38.542
- You feel like you get boxed?
- I don't want it to be, yeah.

00:02:38.542 --> 00:02:39.375
I don't want it 
to be pigeonholed

00:02:39.375 --> 00:02:41.750
into any genre of house.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.625
I just call it house music.

00:02:43.625 --> 00:02:45.375
And it's different at times,

00:02:45.375 --> 00:02:47.583
a bit tribal,
sometimes with African,

00:02:47.583 --> 00:02:51.208
sometimes it's a
bit jazzy, more RnB-ish,

00:02:51.208 --> 00:02:53.417
more house, but
it is house music.

00:02:53.417 --> 00:02:55.125
- So well actually
let's take a step back.

00:02:55.125 --> 00:02:57.792
When did you realize as a
youngster you loved music?

00:02:57.792 --> 00:02:59.083
Was it when you were,

00:02:59.083 --> 00:03:00.750
right away from the womb?

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:01.667
Later in life?

00:03:01.667 --> 00:03:02.750
- When I was a kid,

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:06.083
basically I just
loved dancing to music.

00:03:07.708 --> 00:03:08.917
And then growing up,

00:03:08.917 --> 00:03:10.708
one of my cousins and his friend

00:03:10.708 --> 00:03:13.542
had like a mobile
disco sound system.

00:03:13.542 --> 00:03:14.750
I used to go with them,

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.917
help them connect
the system at parties

00:03:16.917 --> 00:03:21.250
and anything that had
music I would be there.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:24.083
I didn't know what
I really wanted to do.

00:03:24.083 --> 00:03:26.166
When they introduced
the music class in school,

00:03:26.166 --> 00:03:29.000
I was the first
one to go in there.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.250
Still I didn't know.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.250
I went to study,

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:33.834
I listen to the music next door.

00:03:33.834 --> 00:03:35.458
I studied jazz music.

00:03:35.458 --> 00:03:38.083
And even then I didn't
know what I would do.

00:03:38.083 --> 00:03:39.667
But there was a music studio

00:03:39.667 --> 00:03:42.875
which I really loved
working on music production

00:03:42.875 --> 00:03:43.959
more than anything.

00:03:43.959 --> 00:03:46.542
And I think that's
where the production work

00:03:46.542 --> 00:03:49.041
started shaping up.

00:03:49.041 --> 00:03:51.917
- And as you were going
through this music journey

00:03:51.917 --> 00:03:55.000
who were the one, two, three,

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.083
influences you
think in your music life?

00:03:57.083 --> 00:03:59.959
I'm always fascinated
by who people listen to

00:03:59.959 --> 00:04:02.333
or what was the song
or who was the producer.

00:04:02.333 --> 00:04:04.917
What were some of
those keystone individuals

00:04:04.917 --> 00:04:08.250
and/or moments in
your music journey?

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:09.667
- I think as a,

00:04:09.667 --> 00:04:11.542
at an early age

00:04:11.542 --> 00:04:13.750
I just,

00:04:15.792 --> 00:04:19.500
sort of visualized
myself on a global scale.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:22.500
I used to have dreams of then

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.000
working with Michael Jackson,

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.458
as a kid in Africa.

00:04:26.458 --> 00:04:27.750
- Right.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:31.083
- I used to have,
daydream about him

00:04:31.083 --> 00:04:32.208
and me being friends,

00:04:32.208 --> 00:04:34.375
and he would come to
South Africa for his show

00:04:34.375 --> 00:04:35.875
and we'd hang out.

00:04:36.834 --> 00:04:40.750
I think he's one of the people
that played a major role.

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:42.208
I listened to a lot of the stuff

00:04:42.208 --> 00:04:43.917
that he was
doing with Quincy Jones

00:04:43.917 --> 00:04:47.041
very early and
just watched his career

00:04:47.041 --> 00:04:49.041
and many others.

00:04:49.041 --> 00:04:49.917
From an early age

00:04:49.917 --> 00:04:52.041
I've always had that

00:04:52.041 --> 00:04:55.417
global ambition.

00:04:55.417 --> 00:04:56.208
- You know it's funny,

00:04:56.208 --> 00:04:57.458
you have a piece of content

00:04:57.458 --> 00:04:58.583
that caught my attention

00:04:58.583 --> 00:05:00.375
where it's a
drone coming in and out

00:05:00.375 --> 00:05:02.500
and you referenced,

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:03.458
you referenced there

00:05:03.458 --> 00:05:06.542
I think as a kid
I daydreamed a lot,

00:05:06.542 --> 00:05:07.625
I was in my head a lot.

00:05:07.625 --> 00:05:08.458
- Yeah.

00:05:08.458 --> 00:05:09.291
- And this is
now the second time.

00:05:09.291 --> 00:05:10.250
So is that what you were doing?

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:12.333
When you were sitting there,

00:05:12.333 --> 00:05:13.375
is that what
you were just doing?

00:05:13.375 --> 00:05:16.500
You were dreaming it out?

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:17.750
You were picturing

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.166
what it was gonna look like?
- Everything, man.

00:05:19.166 --> 00:05:21.041
- With you and
Michael Jackson being homies?

00:05:21.041 --> 00:05:22.083
- Yeah, man.

00:05:22.083 --> 00:05:23.875
Every, it was
the craziest thing.

00:05:23.875 --> 00:05:24.875
- Where were you guys?

00:05:24.875 --> 00:05:26.708
Were you in
California, in Neverland?

00:05:26.708 --> 00:05:27.875
- That's the thing.

00:05:27.875 --> 00:05:31.333
I was in a
township in South Africa.

00:05:33.417 --> 00:05:36.333
Almost rural place
called the Eastern Cape,

00:05:36.333 --> 00:05:37.750
almost, almost rural.

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:40.458
I say that because
my grandmother had cows

00:05:40.458 --> 00:05:42.959
so I grew up
milking cows literally.

00:05:42.959 --> 00:05:47.125
And on holidays I would
be like the shepherd boy.

00:05:47.125 --> 00:05:48.792
I would look after the cows.

00:05:48.792 --> 00:05:51.417
That's where the
daydreaming was taking place,

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:54.333
in the field when
I'm sitting with the cows

00:05:54.333 --> 00:05:57.125
and that's how I would
spend my holidays.

00:05:57.125 --> 00:05:57.959
- I love that.

00:05:57.959 --> 00:06:00.708
So before we get
into some of the questions

00:06:00.708 --> 00:06:03.917
that are coming
through on social,

00:06:03.917 --> 00:06:07.500
what was kind of the break,

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:08.458
it's obviously been,

00:06:08.458 --> 00:06:10.792
overnight success
takes your whole life,

00:06:10.792 --> 00:06:12.708
but was there a,

00:06:12.708 --> 00:06:14.583
there's a lot of
entrepreneurs that are watching,

00:06:14.583 --> 00:06:16.625
a lot of creators,
a lot of artists,

00:06:16.625 --> 00:06:19.458
I think for everybody
there is always moments.

00:06:19.458 --> 00:06:20.375
It might not,

00:06:20.375 --> 00:06:22.166
for me I never think
of it as the one thing

00:06:22.166 --> 00:06:24.667
but it's been seven or
10 or 11 different things.

00:06:24.667 --> 00:06:26.000
Do you remember the first thing

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.708
that kind of
changed the outcome?

00:06:27.708 --> 00:06:29.000
Obviously you mentioned earlier

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.166
you've tried a
couple things in the past.

00:06:31.166 --> 00:06:32.625
They didn't work out.

00:06:32.625 --> 00:06:34.583
For the thing that did work out,

00:06:34.583 --> 00:06:35.875
did somebody put you on?

00:06:35.875 --> 00:06:37.959
Did somebody play a song?

00:06:37.959 --> 00:06:40.083
What was the moment
where you first tasted,

00:06:40.083 --> 00:06:42.500
okay, there's
something happening here.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:45.375
- I think when I got
accepted to study music

00:06:45.375 --> 00:06:48.500
at a university level.

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.875
I then thought, okay
I might have something.

00:06:50.875 --> 00:06:54.458
'cause it was the
most weird thing for me.

00:06:54.458 --> 00:06:58.708
I can't really sing but they
interviewed me as a singer.

00:06:58.708 --> 00:07:00.041
So they asked me to sing

00:07:00.041 --> 00:07:01.458
and that's how I got in.

00:07:01.458 --> 00:07:03.708
So I majored in jazz voice.

00:07:05.708 --> 00:07:08.208
So that's, for me that was,

00:07:08.208 --> 00:07:10.875
okay, maybe I do have something.

00:07:12.125 --> 00:07:13.250
And then while we were studying,

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:15.792
then we were signed as a band

00:07:15.792 --> 00:07:19.792
by a label from London.

00:07:19.792 --> 00:07:23.291
That was also
another step to say okay,

00:07:23.291 --> 00:07:26.000
we actually do
have something going.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:29.667
Those two steps for me
were the most important,

00:07:29.667 --> 00:07:30.917
in terms of just knowing,

00:07:30.917 --> 00:07:33.542
okay, I don't
know what I'm doing

00:07:33.542 --> 00:07:36.583
but I am in the right direction.

00:07:36.583 --> 00:07:39.291
- What about your
relationship with social?

00:07:39.291 --> 00:07:42.458
You have a really
substantial following.

00:07:42.458 --> 00:07:43.834
Does that come native to you?

00:07:43.834 --> 00:07:46.333
As somebody who's
been sitting with the cows

00:07:46.333 --> 00:07:49.166
and in your own
head as a framework,

00:07:49.166 --> 00:07:52.000
has the outward
aspect of the fame

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.959
or the hundreds of
thousands of millions of fans

00:07:54.959 --> 00:07:56.750
that follow you
on these platforms.

00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:57.875
Has that come natural?

00:07:57.875 --> 00:07:59.333
Is that something
that you've had to develop?

00:07:59.333 --> 00:08:00.291
Do you like it?

00:08:00.291 --> 00:08:02.834
Do you not like it but
you recognize the importance

00:08:02.834 --> 00:08:04.291
in what you do for a living?

00:08:04.291 --> 00:08:06.291
Talk to me about
that relationship.

00:08:06.291 --> 00:08:07.792
- I love that relationship.

00:08:07.792 --> 00:08:10.291
I respect it a lot.

00:08:10.291 --> 00:08:12.333
I see it as a great platform

00:08:12.333 --> 00:08:14.667
to talk to my people a lot.

00:08:14.667 --> 00:08:18.750
Also because I'm hardly
in South Africa any more.

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:22.458
You know, at times
I feel like I'm detached

00:08:22.458 --> 00:08:25.917
from everything that's
happening, from the fan base.

00:08:25.917 --> 00:08:27.583
And for me it's very

00:08:27.583 --> 00:08:31.000
important to be accessible.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:34.375
You know, it's very
important for me to

00:08:34.375 --> 00:08:38.041
talk to them and
make them understand that

00:08:38.041 --> 00:08:41.291
whatever that I'm
doing they can do it.

00:08:42.707 --> 00:08:45.625
So it helps me close
that gap and I'm able to talk.

00:08:45.625 --> 00:08:47.500
Like today my tweet was

00:08:49.375 --> 00:08:52.041
"What did you do today to

00:08:52.041 --> 00:08:56.208
"to get closer to the
life that you deserve?"

00:08:56.208 --> 00:08:58.542
That was my tweet today,
and it got a lot of traction

00:08:58.542 --> 00:09:01.458
because it was a
genuine question.

00:09:01.458 --> 00:09:04.125
And that's what
I try and post about.

00:09:04.125 --> 00:09:07.417
I post about real things.

00:09:07.417 --> 00:09:10.667
There's a line though, you know.

00:09:10.667 --> 00:09:12.750
There's a very thin line,

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:15.417
'cause there's a real life

00:09:15.417 --> 00:09:18.125
and then there's
a real personal life

00:09:18.125 --> 00:09:21.333
and I try separate
the personal things

00:09:21.333 --> 00:09:24.542
'cause I don't think
they'll motivate some people.

00:09:24.542 --> 00:09:28.041
Sometimes they can just
kill their spirits as well.

00:09:28.041 --> 00:09:30.375
So I try to be
as real as possible

00:09:30.375 --> 00:09:33.959
and I use my
social media platforms

00:09:33.959 --> 00:09:38.708
all the time to post
things that can only build.

00:09:38.708 --> 00:09:41.000
'Cause you must
remember, I'm from South Africa

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:45.291
and we have a very
turbulent past as a country.

00:09:45.291 --> 00:09:49.208
And it effected a lot of

00:09:49.208 --> 00:09:54.166
our parents and
our great grandparents.

00:09:54.166 --> 00:09:58.333
And they didn't have
much to give us, you know,

00:09:58.333 --> 00:10:03.041
even in terms of
drive and motivation.

00:10:03.041 --> 00:10:05.208
So we are building as
a country at the moment.

00:10:05.208 --> 00:10:07.041
We try to find our feet,

00:10:07.041 --> 00:10:11.750
and it's always important to

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:14.458
push that message
that, guys remember,

00:10:14.458 --> 00:10:18.875
we are far from where
the rest of the world is.

00:10:18.875 --> 00:10:23.750
I try so hard to use my social
media to communicate such.

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:24.583
- Love it.

00:10:24.583 --> 00:10:28.208
Lindsay is gonna ask the
questions here today DRock.

00:10:28.208 --> 00:10:29.417
So Linds ...

00:10:29.417 --> 00:10:30.667
- Hello, testing.

00:10:30.667 --> 00:10:34.375
Okay, so first the live
stream is a little--

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:37.750
so the live stream
is a little spotty.

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:40.417
- Okay. But do
you have questions?

00:10:40.417 --> 00:10:42.917
- They're all asking
- [Gary] Okay.

00:10:42.917 --> 00:10:46.583
- So Roy (mumbles) wants to
know what's more beneficial

00:10:46.583 --> 00:10:50.750
being a jack of all
trades or being a master of one?

00:10:53.125 --> 00:10:57.542
- For me, growing up,
music was definitely my thing.

00:10:57.542 --> 00:11:00.208
Like this is all I do, you know.

00:11:00.208 --> 00:11:04.875
And, but I have
love for art, you know.

00:11:04.875 --> 00:11:08.708
I've given love
for other things.

00:11:08.708 --> 00:11:11.500
You know, and not everyone

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:15.250
can be a master of everything,

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:16.625
but if you have
the talent I think

00:11:16.625 --> 00:11:20.375
that's what it boils down to.

00:11:20.375 --> 00:11:23.208
To have the talent
of doing other things.

00:11:23.208 --> 00:11:27.000
One of the reasons why Gary

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:28.500
inspires me so much,

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:32.291
is the fact that he's not just

00:11:32.291 --> 00:11:35.208
one minded or

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:40.500
his business is
only on one direction.

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:42.750
You know, he does
so many different things

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:44.583
and he does all
of them with passion

00:11:44.583 --> 00:11:46.750
and I think that's
the most important thing.

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:50.166
I draw so much from him

00:11:50.166 --> 00:11:53.166
and many other people who,

00:11:53.166 --> 00:11:56.125
like Virgil Abloh
who's an architect

00:11:56.125 --> 00:12:00.625
and a clothing designer
and so many other things.

00:12:00.625 --> 00:12:02.500
If you have the
talent and the drive

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:06.667
to be able to
master what you know

00:12:07.959 --> 00:12:10.959
then don't stop
because you are afraid

00:12:10.959 --> 00:12:13.875
to be tagged as
a master of none.

00:12:13.875 --> 00:12:16.792
I just think if the
talent is there, go on.

00:12:16.792 --> 00:12:17.875
- I'll add on that.

00:12:17.875 --> 00:12:21.166
I think self-awareness
matters so much, right?

00:12:21.166 --> 00:12:22.333
Like, I think that there's a
lot--

00:12:22.333 --> 00:12:24.166
I mean I look and
I know a lot of you

00:12:24.166 --> 00:12:26.458
a lot or a
little bit from a far,

00:12:26.458 --> 00:12:28.917
I'm sure we all
aspire for other things

00:12:28.917 --> 00:12:30.458
that we could have
done or wanted to be

00:12:30.458 --> 00:12:31.917
especially when
we're hangin around

00:12:31.917 --> 00:12:33.500
with our friends and
things of that nature,

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:35.750
but the reality is you've
gotta realize what you are.

00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:40.041
And I also think
that one can judge,

00:12:40.041 --> 00:12:43.834
it's subjective if you're
the master of a certain thing

00:12:43.834 --> 00:12:45.208
or you're doin a whole
bunch of different things.

00:12:45.208 --> 00:12:47.750
So, for me,
I appreciate those kind words.

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:49.917
And it's funny for me,
all the things I do,

00:12:49.917 --> 00:12:51.917
and I do so many
different things,

00:12:51.917 --> 00:12:54.250
but ironically, for me,
they're actually just

00:12:54.250 --> 00:12:57.000
a heavy reliance on empathy,

00:12:57.000 --> 00:12:58.834
which then leads to
being a good salesman

00:12:58.834 --> 00:13:01.041
because if you
understand other people

00:13:01.041 --> 00:13:02.792
and what they want
you're deploying against that.

00:13:02.792 --> 00:13:04.959
And then I'm just
kind of looking at culture

00:13:04.959 --> 00:13:08.291
and trends and
deploying those opportunities.

00:13:08.291 --> 00:13:10.500
So I don't know if I'm
doing a jack of all trades

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:12.834
'cause I'm doing, whether
it's wine or advertising

00:13:12.834 --> 00:13:15.500
or whatever the heck I'm doing.

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:17.333
Or am I a master of one which is

00:13:17.333 --> 00:13:20.208
I am so deeply entrenched into

00:13:20.208 --> 00:13:21.291
my emotional intelligence around

00:13:21.291 --> 00:13:23.959
one single thing called empathy

00:13:23.959 --> 00:13:26.417
that is the seed that
reverses all the way back.

00:13:26.417 --> 00:13:29.250
So I think even
the definition of that,

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:31.625
I could see
myself being either, or.

00:13:31.625 --> 00:13:33.083
And I think we
spend so much time

00:13:33.083 --> 00:13:36.000
debating things
like that, and the people

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:37.291
that ask those
kind of questions,

00:13:37.291 --> 00:13:39.291
my answer is always just "Do".

00:13:39.291 --> 00:13:41.375
Sitting around and
pondering the strategy

00:13:41.375 --> 00:13:45.708
of being a master or
a jack instead of doing,

00:13:45.708 --> 00:13:49.583
is a huge mistake. Linds

00:13:49.583 --> 00:13:51.333
- [Lindsay] So on
a personal note,

00:13:51.333 --> 00:13:55.208
we were talking about earlier,
how was your Father's Day?

00:13:55.208 --> 00:13:58.208
- My Father's Day was amazing.

00:13:58.208 --> 00:14:00.792
I was home, actually, last week.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:03.583
So I got the little gifts

00:14:03.583 --> 00:14:06.041
from my little ones
from school, the drawings.

00:14:06.041 --> 00:14:09.542
I attended a Father's Day thing
at the little one's school.

00:14:09.542 --> 00:14:11.792
They got us
singing and jumping around.

00:14:11.792 --> 00:14:14.291
So it was, it
was really amazing.

00:14:14.291 --> 00:14:18.000
- I think the Cannes Film
Festival is a terrible festival

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:21.000
because it's
actually during Father's Day.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.375
And like, literally the fact
that they can't move it a week,

00:14:24.375 --> 00:14:25.000
upsets me greatly.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:27.041
So I have a half a
Father's Day every year

00:14:27.041 --> 00:14:28.959
because I have to jump
on a late plane to get here

00:14:28.959 --> 00:14:30.875
for all this stuff going on, so.

00:14:30.875 --> 00:14:33.542
I'd like to use this
platform right now, to lobby

00:14:33.542 --> 00:14:36.041
for a push back by one week.

00:14:36.041 --> 00:14:39.708
Hope somebody here at
the Lions is listening.

00:14:39.708 --> 00:14:41.500
- [Lindsay] Alright,
this is a very specific

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:44.125
question from
Jesse K., 16 years old,

00:14:44.125 --> 00:14:45.625
from Upper Saddle River,
New Jersey.

00:14:45.625 --> 00:14:46.959
- Yes, Jersey in the house.

00:14:46.959 --> 00:14:50.417
- [Lindsay] Phone
number: 201-888-2730.

00:14:50.417 --> 00:14:53.417
- Wow, sharing info.

00:14:53.417 --> 00:14:54.166
- [Lindsay] Question regarding--

00:14:54.166 --> 00:14:55.333
- Call 'em, freak 'em out.

00:14:55.333 --> 00:14:57.041
- Teen entrepreneurship
and whether you should jump

00:14:57.041 --> 00:14:59.625
at every opportunity,

00:14:59.625 --> 00:15:01.417
when you're
trying to balance school

00:15:01.417 --> 00:15:05.250
and business as
much as possible.

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:07.417
- [Black Coffee] Look,
in my experience,

00:15:07.417 --> 00:15:10.291
I'll say jump, you know.

00:15:10.291 --> 00:15:14.333
The sooner you jump, the better
because you learn so much

00:15:14.333 --> 00:15:17.291
from trying and failing.

00:15:17.291 --> 00:15:19.625
If you don't try now,

00:15:19.625 --> 00:15:22.041
you might just try a
little bit too late, you know.

00:15:22.041 --> 00:15:24.750
So, learn now while you're 16.

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:27.250
By 18 you'll have an
experience of some sort.

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:31.250
You'll know what to take and
what not to take, you know.

00:15:31.250 --> 00:15:33.583
It doesn't matter
how many, you know.

00:15:33.583 --> 00:15:35.250
I'm from that school

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:37.959
that always used to calculate,

00:15:37.959 --> 00:15:40.208
and like no, I'm not ready.

00:15:40.208 --> 00:15:43.375
And with growing up,
I've learned that actually

00:15:43.375 --> 00:15:46.208
I've wasted so much
time on trying to plan things

00:15:46.208 --> 00:15:47.542
that I don't even know.

00:15:47.542 --> 00:15:51.875
You know, so I'd go all-in,
and I'll get that stress

00:15:51.875 --> 00:15:55.250
as I get on with it, basically.

00:15:55.250 --> 00:15:56.667
- I couldn't agree more.

00:15:56.667 --> 00:16:01.500
I'm, there's so much
more up side than down side,

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:03.583
in taking risks at a young age,

00:16:03.583 --> 00:16:04.750
it's just practical.

00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:07.917
It's practical to go
high risk-high reward,

00:16:07.917 --> 00:16:09.542
in your teens and 20s.

00:16:09.542 --> 00:16:11.834
It's just life practical.

00:16:11.834 --> 00:16:14.208
And I think the
reason people don't,

00:16:14.208 --> 00:16:17.708
is they love their
parents, or grandparents,

00:16:17.708 --> 00:16:20.291
or somebody in their life that
they don't want to disappoint

00:16:20.291 --> 00:16:24.250
by doing the thing that
seems crazy to that elder.

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:27.041
And, you know, I don't
want to sit on a pedestal

00:16:27.041 --> 00:16:29.417
and say, you know, do that.

00:16:29.417 --> 00:16:31.583
I understand
what's stopping them.

00:16:31.583 --> 00:16:32.750
I just want to challenge people

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:36.750
to recognize that
regret is very scary.

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:38.458
And what ends up happening is

00:16:38.458 --> 00:16:40.959
you're protecting your
parents' feelings in your 20s

00:16:40.959 --> 00:16:43.250
by doing the thing
you want them to do.

00:16:43.250 --> 00:16:46.500
But how it manifests
is in a relationship

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:48.917
in your 40s, 50s,
and 60s with your parents

00:16:48.917 --> 00:16:52.208
that is less healthy
because you start blaming them

00:16:52.208 --> 00:16:54.166
for not taking those jumps.

00:16:54.166 --> 00:16:57.291
And so I feel like you
can fight with your parents

00:16:57.291 --> 00:16:59.750
now or later, and I just
think it works out much better

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:00.917
in the upfront.

00:17:03.583 --> 00:17:05.666
- [Lindsay] So on the
other end of the spectrum,

00:17:05.666 --> 00:17:07.875
Celeste Stewart,
who's watching all the way

00:17:07.875 --> 00:17:11.165
from Cape Town, South Africa,
wants to know,

00:17:11.165 --> 00:17:14.541
how do you deal with rejection?

00:17:14.541 --> 00:17:16.040
- Um,--
- [Lindsay] Gary's like yes.

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:20.000
- Let me take it first,
and then he can take it.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:21.416
Phenomenally.

00:17:21.416 --> 00:17:22.875
I fuckin' love rejection.

00:17:22.875 --> 00:17:26.165
I'm so blown away by
people's lack of understanding

00:17:26.165 --> 00:17:28.290
that battle scars
are attractive.

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:30.125
Like, losses are sexy.

00:17:30.125 --> 00:17:33.417
Like I love losing.

00:17:33.417 --> 00:17:34.750
I love rejection.

00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:36.375
I actually go the other way.

00:17:36.375 --> 00:17:38.083
When I win, I get weird.

00:17:38.083 --> 00:17:39.750
I hated winning.
- [Black Coffee] Same here.

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:41.583
- You?
- (laughs) Same here.

00:17:43.750 --> 00:17:46.625
Especially me coming
from where I come from,

00:17:46.625 --> 00:17:50.458
you know, I start
feeling bad for the guys that.

00:17:50.458 --> 00:17:51.291
- Didn't make it?

00:17:51.291 --> 00:17:55.500
- Didn't win, and you know,
but this is how I started.

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:59.208
My first album,
which came out in 2005

00:17:59.208 --> 00:18:01.375
was an album of remixes,

00:18:01.375 --> 00:18:02.834
and so I went around,

00:18:02.834 --> 00:18:04.834
did my research, asking the guys

00:18:04.834 --> 00:18:06.375
that were doing
what I was doing,

00:18:06.375 --> 00:18:07.708
or what I was trying to do.

00:18:07.708 --> 00:18:11.208
Is it worth it
doing an album of remixes?

00:18:11.208 --> 00:18:12.333
They were like,
what do you mean?

00:18:12.333 --> 00:18:15.708
I'm like, if I take all
the music that's around,

00:18:15.708 --> 00:18:18.166
and remix it and
re-release it, you know,

00:18:18.166 --> 00:18:20.083
do you think it
would be a success?

00:18:20.083 --> 00:18:22.041
And everyone was
like, come on dude,

00:18:22.041 --> 00:18:24.458
people know the
songs already, you know.

00:18:24.458 --> 00:18:25.875
You're wasting your time.

00:18:25.875 --> 00:18:27.458
This is how I started, you know.

00:18:27.458 --> 00:18:29.458
And I felt very,

00:18:32.458 --> 00:18:35.417
I believed in the idea so much
that I went ahead, you know.

00:18:35.417 --> 00:18:38.250
And released an
album, as my first album,

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:41.375
and I won an
award for it, you know.

00:18:41.375 --> 00:18:43.792
So my debut
album got me an award,

00:18:43.792 --> 00:18:45.750
and it was an album of
remixes that I was told

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:47.583
it was never gonna make it.

00:18:47.583 --> 00:18:48.875
So this is how I started,

00:18:48.875 --> 00:18:52.708
and I feel the same
way about rejection.

00:18:52.708 --> 00:18:54.125
It's always a way for me to say

00:18:54.125 --> 00:18:56.667
I think that I'm
on the right track.

00:18:56.667 --> 00:18:59.000
You know, the fact that a lot
of people aren't understanding

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:02.166
because we live in a world
where everyone is following.

00:19:02.166 --> 00:19:05.500
Everyone is following, and

00:19:05.500 --> 00:19:08.166
we wake up, and it's too late.

00:19:08.166 --> 00:19:10.750
Someone goes the other
way, and they create Facebook.

00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:12.000
You're like wow.

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:13.708
Why didn't we think of that?

00:19:13.708 --> 00:19:15.917
You know I bet you Zuckerberg,

00:19:15.917 --> 00:19:18.250
when he first came with an idea,

00:19:18.250 --> 00:19:20.542
if he had put it
out there that he wanted

00:19:20.542 --> 00:19:21.583
to create such a thing,

00:19:21.583 --> 00:19:24.917
I don't think many people
would have agreed with him.

00:19:24.917 --> 00:19:26.458
And that's how it is, you know.

00:19:26.458 --> 00:19:30.583
I just think, find
power in rejection.

00:19:30.583 --> 00:19:35.208
Use that power to
fuel you to do even better.

00:19:35.208 --> 00:19:38.250
- Yeah I mean, to
me, the way I think,

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:40.834
I just, it's
basically a binary thing.

00:19:40.834 --> 00:19:44.041
I think that
people that are attracted

00:19:44.041 --> 00:19:46.792
and are obsessed
with losing tend to win.

00:19:46.792 --> 00:19:49.291
Like it's such a
counter intuitive thing.

00:19:49.291 --> 00:19:50.542
Like people are
always fascinated

00:19:50.542 --> 00:19:52.375
why I talk on
this subject matter.

00:19:52.375 --> 00:19:54.959
I don't know, I just really,
I love being a Jets fan

00:19:54.959 --> 00:19:57.875
so much, like I love the losses.

00:19:57.875 --> 00:19:58.750
I love the climb.

00:19:58.750 --> 00:20:01.000
It's all the journey.

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:03.041
I mean, and on a personal
level, like that's something

00:20:03.041 --> 00:20:05.250
that I can't control
and that's kind of haha.

00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:08.792
On a personal level, like going
into my dad's liquor store

00:20:08.792 --> 00:20:11.083
like I wasn't good enough
to get a job somewhere else.

00:20:11.083 --> 00:20:15.083
Or going into the advertising
world, or Wine Library TV.

00:20:15.083 --> 00:20:17.500
Being in the wine business,
doing something that everybody.

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:19.792
Everything that I've
ever done in my life,

00:20:19.792 --> 00:20:22.792
has started off with I'm
an idiot, and I'm a loser.

00:20:22.792 --> 00:20:25.583
And ended with I'm a
genius, and I'm a winner.

00:20:25.583 --> 00:20:27.458
And the only way
you can actually do that

00:20:27.458 --> 00:20:29.708
is that you don't
actually give a fuck about

00:20:29.708 --> 00:20:31.000
what anybody else is saying.

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:32.417
Like that's what
it comes down to.

00:20:32.417 --> 00:20:35.333
You're either the kind
of person that crumbles

00:20:35.333 --> 00:20:36.875
at other people's opinions

00:20:36.875 --> 00:20:38.333
about what you're up to,

00:20:38.333 --> 00:20:41.375
or you're the kind of person
that stays in your own head,

00:20:41.375 --> 00:20:43.375
in a fuckin' field with cows,

00:20:43.375 --> 00:20:45.667
or just in your
own head in your bed,

00:20:45.667 --> 00:20:47.625
and realize, I got this.

00:20:47.625 --> 00:20:50.458
And it's gonna taste
so much, like I live for

00:20:50.458 --> 00:20:51.792
I fuckin' told you.

00:20:51.792 --> 00:20:52.834
I live for that.

00:20:52.834 --> 00:20:55.208
Like I don't mind if
people think that's like weird

00:20:55.208 --> 00:20:57.083
and like, it's the best feeling.

00:20:57.083 --> 00:20:59.875
I love looking at
people's faces that didn't think

00:20:59.875 --> 00:21:02.792
and rolling up, like
I've got a sports agency now.

00:21:02.792 --> 00:21:03.959
My brother and I just started.

00:21:03.959 --> 00:21:05.583
And we're trying
to sign these kids.

00:21:05.583 --> 00:21:06.959
And they're going
with much bigger firms,

00:21:06.959 --> 00:21:08.625
as they should, I get it.

00:21:08.625 --> 00:21:10.208
But if you don't
think I can't wait

00:21:10.208 --> 00:21:12.500
to Super Bowl in four years
or the ESPYs in two years,

00:21:12.500 --> 00:21:15.542
and roll up on these
kids and say you fucked up.

00:21:15.542 --> 00:21:18.708
Like that's what's driving
me to take bullshit flights

00:21:18.708 --> 00:21:20.917
to Toledo to meet
their parents right now.

00:21:20.917 --> 00:21:23.625
Literally, just the
fact that most people think

00:21:23.625 --> 00:21:28.166
I'm too busy doing too
many things, and I can't.

00:21:28.166 --> 00:21:30.792
So I deal with it tremendously.

00:21:33.041 --> 00:21:34.667
- [Lindsay] So very relevant

00:21:34.667 --> 00:21:37.708
to what
Steve Outwell says, or asks,

00:21:37.708 --> 00:21:40.792
when you hit your
goals, how do you actually deal

00:21:40.792 --> 00:21:42.166
with feeling worthy of them?

00:21:42.166 --> 00:21:44.834
Which I think you just
answered, but it's a good point.

00:21:44.834 --> 00:21:46.792
- I hate hitting goals.

00:21:46.792 --> 00:21:49.375
Which is why I try not to
make them, other than buying.

00:21:49.375 --> 00:21:51.792
Like I genuinely believe the
day I buy the New York Jets

00:21:51.792 --> 00:21:54.041
is going to be a
weird day for me.

00:21:54.041 --> 00:21:56.750
Like I don't, like I don't know.

00:21:56.750 --> 00:21:58.959
I don't like hitting goals,
I don't like it.

00:21:58.959 --> 00:21:59.917
I actually don't make goals.

00:21:59.917 --> 00:22:02.458
I've got one that's on
the record, it's a big one,

00:22:02.458 --> 00:22:04.750
it's gonna take me some time.

00:22:04.750 --> 00:22:07.083
You?

00:22:07.083 --> 00:22:11.250
- It's a weird
feeling for me as well.

00:22:11.250 --> 00:22:12.291
I don't know, I think--

00:22:12.291 --> 00:22:13.792
- It's a downer, right?

00:22:13.792 --> 00:22:16.291
- the journey is more exciting.

00:22:16.291 --> 00:22:18.875
You know, not knowing
what's going to happen for me,

00:22:18.875 --> 00:22:20.750
it all is exciting.

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:23.583
Like waking up, not sure if
it's going to work out or not,

00:22:23.583 --> 00:22:24.500
but try.

00:22:24.500 --> 00:22:27.333
You know we keep
moving forward, you know.

00:22:27.333 --> 00:22:29.417
I mean in all interviews,

00:22:30.542 --> 00:22:32.041
for the past 5, 6 years,

00:22:32.041 --> 00:22:33.708
I've been mentioning one name.

00:22:33.708 --> 00:22:36.250
And like, who do you
want to collaborate with?

00:22:36.250 --> 00:22:38.166
I'm like, Drake.

00:22:38.166 --> 00:22:40.458
But they like, but
you doing house music.

00:22:40.458 --> 00:22:43.000
I'm like, I also don't
know how its gonna happen.

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:44.834
But that's what I want.

00:22:44.834 --> 00:22:48.583
You know, and why,
that I've always been saying

00:22:48.583 --> 00:22:51.291
because I feel the kind
of house music that we do

00:22:51.291 --> 00:22:56.000
is quite beautiful, but it
it's not accessible to all.

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:58.125
It doesn't have

00:22:58.125 --> 00:23:00.375
that Beyonce
or the Drake budget.

00:23:00.375 --> 00:23:02.959
So, it doesn't get
to many ears, you know.

00:23:02.959 --> 00:23:06.041
But I'm here to wait
and try and make it work.

00:23:06.041 --> 00:23:08.750
Until we got the call
from Drake's people one day,

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:10.625
saying let's do it.

00:23:10.625 --> 00:23:13.041
You know, and
when that happened,

00:23:13.041 --> 00:23:14.875
I couldn't even share it.

00:23:14.875 --> 00:23:18.625
Even though it's been something
I've been chasing for years.

00:23:18.625 --> 00:23:20.417
- Did you have the
feeling where you were pumped

00:23:20.417 --> 00:23:21.959
for like three minutes
and then just like,

00:23:21.959 --> 00:23:23.959
kind of moved forward?

00:23:23.959 --> 00:23:27.834
- Of course, and people
that would be around me,

00:23:27.834 --> 00:23:30.875
my wife,
my friends, they all say,

00:23:30.875 --> 00:23:33.250
but you don't look excited.

00:23:33.250 --> 00:23:34.500
Are you happy?

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:37.083
And I would say
yeah I'm, you know.

00:23:37.083 --> 00:23:41.417
But I think what makes me
happy is the journey, you know.

00:23:41.417 --> 00:23:43.750
- It's the next
thing, every time.

00:23:43.750 --> 00:23:45.083
- Yep, yep.

00:23:45.083 --> 00:23:46.208
- One more Linds.

00:23:46.208 --> 00:23:47.917
